WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:01.847
Task statement 2.5.

2
00:00:01.847 --> 00:00:07.660
Select and apply built-in tools (Read, Write, Edit, Bash, Grep, Glob) effectively.

3
00:00:07.660 --> 00:00:16.735
Exploring an unfamiliar codebase is mostly choosing the right built-in tool for each step, and the wrong one burns context fast.

4
00:00:18.135 --> 00:00:20.783
Claude Code ships with a small toolbox.

5
00:00:20.783 --> 00:00:22.006
Read opens a file.

6
00:00:22.006 --> 00:00:24.791
Write creates or overwrites a whole file.

7
00:00:24.791 --> 00:00:27.168
Edit swaps one exact piece of text.

8
00:00:27.168 --> 00:00:28.458
Bash runs commands.

9
00:00:28.458 --> 00:00:33.960
Grep searches what is inside files, while Glob finds files by their path or name.

10
00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.103
Most exploration mistakes come from mixing up those last two.

11
00:00:39.503 --> 00:00:42.511
Glob answers which files exist, by name pattern.

12
00:00:42.511 --> 00:00:45.206
Grep answers which files mention something.

13
00:00:45.206 --> 00:00:53.102
Edit works by exact text match, and the old text must appear only once, so include a line or two of context to make it unique.

14
00:00:53.102 --> 00:00:56.549
If that fails, read the file and write it back in full.

15
00:00:56.549 --> 00:01:01.625
Current docs note that on Mac and Linux, search now runs through Bash by default.

16
00:01:03.025 --> 00:01:05.178
A good exploration is narrow first.

17
00:01:05.178 --> 00:01:09.977
Grep for the entry point, read just those files, and follow the calls outward.

18
00:01:09.977 --> 00:01:18.712
When a function is wrapped or re-exported under another name, find the exported names first, then grep for each one, or you will miss callers.

19
00:01:18.712 --> 00:01:22.588
Reading every file in a folder is the slow, context-hungry way.

20
00:01:23.988 --> 00:01:25.526
Here is a realistic flow.

21
00:01:25.526 --> 00:01:27.863
Glob shows the billing folder's shape.

22
00:01:27.863 --> 00:01:31.861
Grep for the function name finds where it lives and who calls it.

23
00:01:31.861 --> 00:01:33.091
Read the definition.

24
00:01:33.091 --> 00:01:37.212
If it is wrapped and exported under a new name, grep that name too.

25
00:01:37.212 --> 00:01:42.932
Then make the change with Edit using a unique anchor, and run the tests with Bash to confirm.

26
00:01:44.332 --> 00:01:47.134
Exam distractors here swap the tools around.

27
00:01:47.134 --> 00:01:48.535
Glob cannot find text.

28
00:01:48.535 --> 00:01:50.891
Reading everything is not a strategy.

29
00:01:50.891 --> 00:01:55.284
Edit will keep failing on a repeated anchor until you make it unique.

30
00:01:55.284 --> 00:02:01.143
And tracing through wrappers means searching every exported name, not just the original one.

31
00:02:02.543 --> 00:02:03.115
To recap.

32
00:02:03.115 --> 00:02:05.596
Know which tool answers which question.

33
00:02:05.596 --> 00:02:07.823
Grep for text, Glob for file names.

34
00:02:07.823 --> 00:02:11.894
Make Edit anchors unique, and when that fails, read and rewrite.

35
00:02:11.894 --> 00:02:20.164
Explore by finding entry points first and reading outward, and chase wrapped functions through every name they are exported under.

